<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    **One styles file for every API level, where there used to be three.**

    `values-v31/styles.xml` existed because Android 12 draws its own splash
    window that cannot be turned off, and its attributes have no equivalent
    below 31; `values-night/styles.xml` existed before that and had to be
    deleted, because Android ranks the `night` qualifier above `vNN` and a dark
    phone on Android 12 would silently resolve the wrong one. Both problems
    were the same problem: the launch screen was described in more than one
    place, and the places disagreed by configuration.

    `androidx.core:core-splashscreen` is what collapses them. It backports the
    platform splash attributes to API 21 and reads the same four items on every
    version, so this file is the whole launch screen — one description, no
    qualifier to lose a race with.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
        The launch window, and on Android 12+ the *platform's* splash window
        too — `installSplashScreen()` in MainActivity maps these onto
        `windowSplashScreen*` there and draws them itself below it.

        `postSplashScreenTheme` is what the activity wears afterwards, applied
        by the library. Flutter has its own mechanism for that — the
        `io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme` meta-data in the manifest —
        and it has been **removed**, deliberately: two things swapping the same
        theme at two different moments is a race with nothing to gain from
        winning it.
    -->
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_mark</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/NormalTheme</item>

        <!--
            **Both are honest, and on at least one vendor neither is obeyed.**
            Written down because the next person will otherwise spend an
            afternoon rediscovering it.

            A smart force-dark inverts light neutral backgrounds and leaves
            chroma alone, and `android:forceDarkAllowed` is the documented way
            out — but the platform only consults it when `isLightTheme` is true,
            so the two have to be set together or the opt-out is not read at all.
            They are set together here.

            On a Xiaomi with the vendor's own dark mode on it makes no
            difference. Measured on the launch window with the app's night mode
            pinned light: `#F4F4F4` reached the screen as `#212121`, while a
            probe of pure red reached it as `#FE0000` — so the attribute *is*
            being honoured and something downstream is repainting the result.
            Setting `isLightTheme` false instead, which is the other thing
            people try, changed nothing. The app itself is untouched throughout,
            because Flutter draws into a SurfaceView, and that asymmetry is what
            identified it.

            There is no third attribute. What closes the seam is on the Flutter
            side: `SplashChannel.sampleField` reads the pixel the window is
            actually showing and hands it to `SplashCurtain`, so the handover
            matches whatever the device decided to paint, and the field then
            travels to the app's real background. See both files.

            `windowLightStatusBar` tracks the field separately, because it is a
            different question with a different answer: it decides the *icons*,
            and dark icons on a near-white field is simply correct.

            All of it is stated outright rather than inherited from the parent —
            `Theme.SplashScreen` is the parent now and the library chooses what
            that descends from, so relying on the ancestor would be relying on
            somebody else's decision.
        -->
        <item name="android:isLightTheme" tools:targetApi="29">@bool/splash_field_is_light</item>
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed" tools:targetApi="29">false</item>

        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">@bool/splash_field_is_light</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        What the activity wears once the splash is done with. **A flat colour,
        not artwork.**

        It carried the launch drawable for a while, because the platform hands
        its splash off as soon as the activity can draw — which is long before
        Dart has finished starting — and a flat field for the rest of that is a
        launch screen nobody sees. Holding the artwork here was the only lever
        available at the time.

        There is a better one now: `setKeepOnScreenCondition` holds the real
        splash until Flutter's first frame, and that frame is `SplashCurtain`
        drawing the same mark in the same place. So nothing needs to be
        impersonated here, and this goes back to being what it should be — the
        colour behind the Flutter surface, which is the same colour the curtain
        paints, so any gap a resize opens is invisible instead of a torn piece
        of artwork.
    -->
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <!-- Tracks the field, paired with the opt-out below. See LaunchTheme. -->
        <item name="android:isLightTheme" tools:targetApi="29">@bool/splash_field_is_light</item>
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed" tools:targetApi="29">false</item>

        <!--
            **The system bars, and leaving them out was a visible bug.**

            This theme is what the activity wears from the moment the splash
            hands over until the app's own `SystemChrome` call lands, and its
            parent paints both bars black. The launch window had said
            `splash_background` for both, so the handover turned a strip at the
            top and another at the bottom black while the field between them did
            not move — the one moment in the whole sequence that is supposed to
            be invisible, and the only part of it the eye could actually catch.

            Caught on a device rather than reasoned about, in a frame-by-frame
            capture of a cold start. It is exactly the class of thing a
            screenshot of the finished app never shows.
        -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">@bool/splash_field_is_light</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        Translucent on purpose: the sheet slides up over the app the user was
        already in, and that app has to stay visible behind the scrim.
        windowIsTranslucent is what FlutterFragmentActivity's transparent
        background mode requires to actually take effect.
    -->
    <style name="ShareTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <!--
          Transparent, and it has to stay that way. A placeholder drawable was
          tried here to cover the ~2s cold start with something that looks like
          the sheet, and it never appeared: FlutterActivity's
          configureWindowForTransparency() replaces the window background with
          a transparent ColorDrawable at runtime whenever BackgroundMode is
          transparent. Nothing set here survives that.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowContentOverlay">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
        <!-- The share sheet has no splash of its own: it opens over whatever
             app the user was already in, and a branded field flashing behind a
             translucent sheet would be the same bug in a different window.
             Stated here rather than in a `values-v31` twin now that there is no
             such file — the attribute simply does not resolve below 31. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground" tools:targetApi="31">@android:color/transparent</item>
    </style>
</resources>
